{{wikipedia|valerian (plant)}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|fro|valeriane}} or {{der|en|ML.|valeriāna}}, a reinterpretation of what is found as {{cog|de|Baldrian}} after {{m|la|valeo|valēre|t=to be powerful}} or also the gentilic name {{m|la|Valerius}}, which is seemingly borrowed in the {{w|Migration Period|Dark Age period}} from the late 6<sup>th</sup> to early 8<sup>th</sup> century from {{der|en|trk}} or {{der|en|xgn-pro|-}}, when the {{w|Pannonian Avars}} were direct neighbours to the Germans, notably also present in {{cog|hu|bojtorján|t=burdock}}, ultimately from {{der|en|xgn-pro|-}}, reflected as<br/>
{{cog|xng|ᠪᠠᠯᠴᠢᠷᠭᠠᠨ᠎ᠠ|t=[[false hellebore]]; [[angelica]]}}, composed as {{m|xng|ᠪᠠᠯᠴᠢᠷ|t=infant; young, tender, fresh, rank}} + plant name suffix {{m|xng| ᠭᠠᠨᠠ}},<br/>
{{cog|mn|балчиргана|t=[[false hellebore]]; [[angelica]]}}, composed as {{m|mn|балчир|t=infant; young, tender, fresh, rank}} + plant name suffix {{m|mn|-гана}}.


See {{cog|ota|بالدران|tr=baldıran|t=hemlock}} for {{cog|trk|-}} cognates.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/vəˈlɪəɹɪən/}}
* {{homophones|en|Valyrian}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[hardy]] [[perennial]] [[flowering plant]], ''[[Valeriana officinalis]]'', with heads of [[sweet]]ly [[scent]]ed pink or white [[flower]]s.
# More generally, any plant of the genus ''[[Valeriana]]''.
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The root of ''[[Valeriana officinalis]]'', used in herbal medicine.

